secure-terminal-cli(1) -- sanitizing terminal wrapper for the command line
==========================================================================

<!--
# Copyright (C) 2026 - 2026 ENCRYPTED SUPPORT LLC <adrelanos@whonix.org>
# See the file COPYING for copying conditions.
-->

## SYNOPSIS

`secure-terminal-cli` [`--mode` <MODE>] [<COMMAND> [<ARGS>...]]

## DESCRIPTION

secure-terminal-cli is the same safe core as `secure-terminal`(1) without the
GUI. It runs a command (or your login shell) in a pseudo-terminal and sanitizes
the program's output before it reaches your existing terminal: escape sequences
are removed and every byte that is not printable ASCII is neutralized, so a
crafted file, log or SSH banner cannot repaint the screen, reach a line already
scrolled past, set the window title or touch the clipboard. Safe colour and four
line-local edits (cursor forward/back, cursor-to-column, erase-in-line) are
honored within the line being written, so a program can still redraw its own
current line, exactly as a carriage return does.

Unlike the GUI, it does not sanitize your keystrokes and does not run the paste
warning: it makes what a program *displays* safe, on a plain console or inside an
SSH session that has no access to the GUI. It does not sandbox the program you
run; if you choose to run something harmful, it runs, exactly as in any terminal.

## OPTIONS

  * `--mode` <MODE>:
    How to show non-ASCII output. `detail` (the safe default) names every
    non-ASCII character inline as `<U+XXXX NAME>`, so a look-alike reads as its
    identity; `box` replaces non-ASCII with `_` (the command-line wrapper writes
    to an outer terminal, so it has no colour to tint the box with); `show`
    renders legitimate printable glyphs, including homoglyphs that the outer
    terminal cannot tell from ASCII, while still neutralizing the invisible, bidi
    and control classes to `_`; `reveal` shows every non-ASCII character as a
    `<U+XXXX>` badge.

  * <COMMAND> [<ARGS>...]:
    The command to run. With no command, your login shell is started.

  * `-h`, `--help`:
    Show a usage summary and exit.

